explanation:
The body takes the `NormedField L` structure `spectralNorm.normedField K L`, whose norm is `spectralNorm K L` and whose distance is `spectralNorm K L (x - y)`. It then proves nontriviality. If `x ∈ K` has `1 < ‖x‖`, then `algebraMap K L x` has the same spectral norm, because the spectral norm extends the norm of K (`spectralNorm_extends`). So L with the spectral norm is a nontrivially normed field.

The hypotheses are the standard ones in the literature for the spectral norm to be a field norm extending that of K:
- K is complete;
- K is ultrametric;
- L is algebraic over K.

`NontriviallyNormedField K` is exactly what nontriviality on L needs. So the definition is not too narrow. Mathlib has no other definition of this object; it is the canonical one.
